[{:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-20909"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062586/assets/59704c6b8e643337113ab99b22eaeaac8d59ac3c15042409e8a0a8eac6ee54fd.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\frac{t}{2},\\,y={e}^{t}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ (shown above)?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-21353"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062586/assets/4aa2bca8faab077c47fb968882579233babe7d323ba3120088e8ea5599fbd570.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={e}^{t},\\,y=t$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=2$ (shown above)?" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-351427/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer/id #uuid "a2a544df-0dd7-541a-b568-b09c9dd797de" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351427/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "19f17a84-21e0-5791-9609-4570686d7a8e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351427/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6" :answer-field/choices ["q-351427/field-1/29ec2dfefa1bd3742bf5ba922b9c261dd5f2325bd8484e9153eb979dbd6eced6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351427" :question/id #uuid "f9d562d1-03f9-5f12-bdf4-8339d2f4180f" :question/math-academy-id "q-351427" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062586/assets/33939aec3ac1315771d36714d15b590980197aabf3c169cdea1a1108baf6d0fa.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2,\\,y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{3}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+2) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-4) \\\\  & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+9{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{9}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /3}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =3t\\,{|}_{0}^{\\pi /3} \\\\  & =3(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-0) \\\\  & =\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-351427/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351427"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-351428/field-1/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer/id #uuid "28a65ab5-d2a6-5f1f-a13a-00e8a2c30bde" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351428/field-1/f1ed4ae8bc05ff3d3ca663428f24329881849c09105a9a883f89c4bc091ce8a5" :answer/id #uuid "b0cf1408-3626-579d-bd57-6c5a99002beb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-351428/field-1" :answer-field/id #uuid "469eef24-1ff0-5680-b2c3-588a90184b2e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/blank :answer-field/correct "q-351428/field-1/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" :answer-field/choices ["q-351428/field-1/82a873b6b3ece3b4059b2da1839886387bbd4d4e56d0ad136fc169204f9d3030" "q-351428/field-1/f1ed4ae8bc05ff3d3ca663428f24329881849c09105a9a883f89c4bc091ce8a5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-351428" :question/id #uuid "97032317-f655-5a61-b128-b1a25789c152" :question/math-academy-id "q-351428" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062586/assets/da2526ff7031c9dbc704b59e60c65df14dcd0678427e02c386fc16a86e1fc89c.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3,\\,y=5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,t\\in [\\frac{\\pi }{5},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-3) \\\\  & =5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1) \\\\  & =-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{(5\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{cos}^{2}⁡t+25{sin}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5{\\int }_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2}\\text{d}t \\\\  & =5t\\,{|}_{\\pi /5}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =5(\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{5}) \\\\  & =5(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-351428/field-1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-351428"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-351439"] :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062586/assets/0b9c4ebc430e05ef205e3d8108f2e1649bbc51b1a6b54e8f47b48e7ddad1e9a6.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $L$ defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=4{cos}^{3}⁡t,\\,y=4{sin}^{3}⁡t,\\,t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{4}].\n$$\n\nDo not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
